{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And there were men.</b> It is the way of Scripture to use singular language with plural." "<b>And there were men.</b> Individuals making Pesach Sheni, not the congregation, and on the day the Tabernacle was erected it was stated, as we learned in Gittin." "<b>Who were impure.</b> Rabbi Yitzchak says: could the bearers of the coffin of Yosef become pure? And could the bearers of the coffins of Nadav and Avihu become pure? For on the first of Nisan the Tabernacle was erected, on the second Elazar burned the red heifer, and they said in Gittin that eight passages were stated on the day the Tabernacle was erected, and the passage of the red heifer is one of them. And on that very day the Tabernacle was erected was Rosh Chodesh Nisan, for on Rosh Chodesh Nadav and Avihu died, as explained in the portion of Shemini. Therefore they could have sprinkled upon them from the waters of the chatat that were prepared on the second of Nisan, to purify all those impure through a corpse, from Rosh Chodesh Nisan until Pesach, so they could offer their Paschal sacrifices in purity. Rather, to whom were they impure? They became impure through a met mitzvah." "<b>And they could not make the Pesach.</b> Because an impure person is not permitted to eat sacrificial foods, as it is written in the portion of Tzav: ״והנפש אשר תאכל מבשר זבח השלמים אשר לה׳ וטומאתו עליו״ etc." "<b>On that day.</b> They cannot offer it — but tomorrow they can. From this we derive that the seventh day of their impurity fell on the eve of Pesach."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:17:10.103304045Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}